Cursor Rules: Mobile (React Native + TypeScript)

Project shape
- Type: React Native app using TypeScript
- Entry: index.js -> App.tsx
- Native projects: ios/ (Xcode), android/ (Gradle); avoid editing unless necessary
- Package manager: npm only (package-lock.json present)

How to run
- iOS: npm run ios
- Android: npm run android
- Metro: npm start

Conventions
- Prefer functional components and hooks
- Strongly type props and state; use .tsx/.ts
- Platform-specific files (*.ios.tsx, *.android.tsx) only when required
- Co-locate styles; use StyleSheet or existing styling system
- Keep state simple; introduce global state only if requested

Assets & modules
- Use assets/ for images; import with require/URI
- Avoid Node-only APIs; use RN-compatible libraries
- For native modules, run pods in ios/ and ensure Android builds, but do not commit build outputs

Boundaries
- Scope changes to mobile/ only
- Do not commit node_modules, ios/build, DerivedData, android/build, .metro-cache
- Keep .env out of git; maintain .env.example
- When given a task, focus only on that task and avoid unrelated changes
- If, while performing a task, these rules need updating to reflect the latest scenario, update this file as part of the task

Navigation & structure
- If adding navigation, use React Navigation; keep navigators in navigation/
- Place screens/ and components/ appropriately; share logic via hooks/ or services/

Testing
- Use Jest + @testing-library/react-native for tests under __tests__/ or alongside files

Code style
- Respect eslint/prettier; avoid unrelated formatting changes
- Prioritize readability; avoid deep prop drilling (prefer context/hooks)
